WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.890
There are plenty of moving scenes in Avengers:
Endgame but we sat down and came up with our

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.880
list of the moments that hit us the hardest.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:11.080
Grab a box of tissues and settle in we're
bringing you our rankings for the most heartbreaking

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.300
Endgame moments.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.660
First up, we have Black Widow's recruitment
of Clint Barton.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.720
Old Hawkeye isn't handling the loss of his
family who, if you'll recall, were dusted

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:22.440
by Thanos following that snap particularly
well.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.240
He's off traveling around the world, fighting
gangsters and dispensing his own brand of

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.400
cruel justice.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:31.640
So when he reunites with his old crime-fighting
partner, it's a poignant moment.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.039
Especially after the scene earlier in the
movie where a teary-eyed Natasha asks Rhodey

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:38.609
to continue searching for Barton, even though
they both know they're not going to like what

00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:39.100
they find.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.580
Romanoff's devotion to one of her truest and
oldest friends is impressive, as she follows

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.840
Barton's bloody trail across the Pacific all
the way to Japan.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.570
All of this comes to a head in their brief
exchange when she finally confronts him in

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.720
the rainy streets of Tokyo and tells him that
they might've found a way to reverse Thanos'

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.220
Decimation.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.500
The utter despair on Barton's face is just
as heart-wrenching as the pain and empathy

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.480
on Natasha's as she looks at the cold, heartless
killer that her friend has become.

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:07.680
And yet, her belief in redemption is strong,
and it's clear that she doesn't consider him

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:08.680
to be truly lost.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.900
Thor is an emotional guy.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.370
It doesn't matter if he's raging, laughing,
or crying the dude feels all the feels.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.580
So it's hard not to get upset when Thor and
his friends pay a quick visit to Thanos early

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:20.320
on in the film.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.520
During the events that follow, the Mad Titan
reveals that he's reduced the Infinity Stones

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.840
to atoms, and that it has become impossible
to reverse the snap.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.800
Realizing the utter finality of the action,
a glassy-eyed Thor well …

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.040
"What… what did you do?"

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.320
"I went for the head."

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.920
The emotion of the scene is palpable, especially
considering the fact that Thor rarely, if

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.520
ever, kills in cold blood like that.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.490
Sure, he's always up for a brawl, whatever
the reason, but vengefully striking an enemy

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.800
when they're defenseless just isn't his style.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.780
The scene is upsetting enough on its own,
but knowing that it leads to an entire spiral

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.740
of depression and despondency gives it that
much more gravity.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:03.200
While we've seen Marvel torture Thor for years
now, it isn't until Endgame that the Asgardian

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.500
truly breaks, and it's difficult to watch.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.299
Early in the film, Captain Marvel retrieves
Tony Stark and Nebula after they've been drifting

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:11.650
through space for weeks.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.720
The Tesseract-infused hero quickly flies the
pair of survivors back to the Avengers compound

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.220
on Earth.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.420
As they exit the ship, Captain America comes
running up to help his friend, and the two

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.480
come face to face for the first time since
the disastrous ending of Captain America:

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.040
Civil War.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.840
They both explain that neither of them could
stop Thanos.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:32.620
Then Stark, his lip quivering, drops a bombshell
that totally obliterated our hearts, when

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.580
he tells Cap, "I lost the kid."

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.459
Of course, he's referring to Peter Parker,
who was dusted right in his arms in one of

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.140
the most tragic moments of all of Avengers:
Infinity War.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.820
For all of Tony Stark's other cares and concerns,
it's Peter that's at the front of his mind.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.030
Fans absolutely adored their master-apprentice,
father-son dynamic, so Tony's devastated face

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.720
makes for one of the most moving moments in
the entire story.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.720
While Thor's arc throughout Endgame ends on
a positive note, he certainly experiences

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.020
more than one emotional moment along the way.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.969
One of those takes place when he and Rocket
travel back to Asgard in 2013 to retrieve

00:03:05.969 --> 00:03:06.460
Aether.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.580
While Rocket eventually heads off in search
of the angry sludge, Thor completely breaks

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.420
down and runs from the entire situation.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.780
He doesn't get far, though, before he's confronted
by his mother, Frigga, who immediately sees

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.620
that this is her son from the future.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.980
The two end up having a long talk together
during which Frigga reignites Thor's passion

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.060
and convinces him to stop living in apathy.

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:28.720
The scene would be heartwarming on its own,
but the heartbreaking part of it all is the

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:33.130
fact that it takes place right before Frigga
is going to be killed by Dark Elves and the

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.200
Odinson knows it.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.980
Moments from death, she takes the time to
talk to him and give him exactly the kind

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:41.919
of encouragement that only a mother can deliver,
helping him to rise to the challenges he's

00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:46.389
facing and rediscover that, for all of his
failures, shortcomings, and lapses in judgment,

00:03:46.389 --> 00:03:48.029
he is indeed still worthy.

00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:53.299
Not long after Stark's moving last words which
come via a hologram projection from his helmet,

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:56.819
because Tony Stark the scene shifts to the
porch of his country home, where the ever-faithful

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.220
Happy Hogan sits with Tony's daughter, Morgan.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.940
The two have a little conversation that turns
into a heartbreaking nostalgia-fest.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.180
Happy asks Morgan if she wants something to
eat, to which she replies, “Cheeseburgers.”

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.279
Now, that might just sound like a kid who's
too young to understand what's going on naming

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.820
their favorite food — but when Happy tells
young Morgan …

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.540
"You know, your dad liked cheeseburgers."

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.840
… it sure feels like a punch in the gut.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.440
The callback to Tony Stark's first meal after
escaping captivity in Iron Man is immediately

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.980
recognizable, and for the poor audience watching,
Happy's line makes the tears flow that much

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:27.480
more.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:32.880
As Avengers: Endgame approaches its climactic
conclusion, an exchange takes place between

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.810
the children of Thanos that contains one of
the saddest moments of the entire film.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:41.160
After Clint Barton successfully escapes from
the rubble of the demolished Avengers facility,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:41.980
he meets Nebula.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.460
Taking her for a friend, he lets her have
the Iron Man Gauntlet, only to find out moments

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.750
later that rather than the current 2023 Nebula
who's aiding the heroes, this is the 2014

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:55.460
version, a Nebula who's still emphatically
loyal to her Titan father.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:05:01.340
Suddenly, 2023 Nebula and a recently reformed
2014 Gamora enter the scene, saving Barton's

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:05.350
skin and turning the entire situation into
a timey-wimey family feud.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:10.090
Gamora pleads with her past sister while 2023
Nebula points out that Thanos is the one that

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.400
made them the way they are.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.820
For all the entreaties, though, past Nebula
reveals that she's trapped by what Thanos

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:16.400
has made her.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.920
She then aims at Gamora, only to be shot by
her future self before she can pull the trigger.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.240
Tony Stark loves leaving messages in the event
of an untimely death.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.220
Yeah, sure, he does it near the end of the
film after he actually dies, but it wasn't

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.090
quite as heartbreaking as the other one he
makes earlier in the film.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:36.740
Facing a cold, dark death in the void of space,
Mr. Stark turns on his helmet in order to

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.870
record a final message for his fiancée, Pepper
Potts, before he runs out of oxygen.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:44.630
He keeps the tone of the conversation light,
but even he knows the message is going to

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.020
be "a real tearjerker."

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.260
The scene is tough to watch on multiple levels.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.220
Seeing an already defeated man prepare to
die while faced with the infinite blackness

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.250
of space, all while thinking about the love
of his life … well, it's quite a way to

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:57.240
kick off a movie.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:02.040
During the sequence when Tony Stark and Steve
Rogers head back to the '70s in order to get

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:06.280
the Tesseract and some more Pym Particles,
Rogers finds himself being hunted inside an

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.360
underground bunker.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.400
He ducks into a side door in order to avoid
detection and finds himself in the office

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:15.710
of none other than Peggy Carter, the love
of his life before he "went in the ice back

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:16.460
in '45."

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.440
As he realizes this, he suddenly sees Miss
Carter enter the next room.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.890
His face is a study in heartbreak as he stands
mere feet from the woman that he's never stopped

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.960
loving, knowing that his mission, his friends,
and the fate of half of the universe are preventing

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.220
him from revealing himself.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.050
Cap's story throughout the MCU heavily revolves
around feeling misplaced, and this scene sums

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:39.360
that up in one epically tragic expression,
filled with a longing for something he knows

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.120
he can't have.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:43.660
Thank goodness he finally gets that dance
with Peggy once all's said and done.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:48.200
After the moving arrival of Stark and Nebula
back on Earth in the opening moments of the

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.450
film, the heroes regroup to figure out how
to track down Thanos and reverse the snap.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:56.620
But during the meeting, Stark lashes out at
Steve Rogers with all of the pent-up frustration

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:00.200
and anger that's been building up since the
two came to blows in Captain America: Civil

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:00.700
War.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:01.880
"I said we'd lose.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.050
You said, 'We'll do that together too.'

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:06.140
Well, guess what, Cap?

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:07.500
We lost.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.360
And you weren't there."

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:14.260
During his outburst, Stark accuses Rogers
of not being there when he needed him, adding

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:17.820
that he doesn't trust him anymore before calling
the star-spangled soldier a liar.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:22.860
The duo's reunion scene had been hotly anticipated
for a long time, as Civil War had come nearly

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.500
three years before, and the two hadn't come
face to face again until this point.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:30.090
While everyone knew the get-together wouldn't
be a pretty one, the burst of frustration

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.840
and the evident feeling of betrayal from Stark
ends up being as miserable as possible.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.900
At this point, it's pretty clear that Endgame
is filled with heartbreak from one end to

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:39.400
the other.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.040
In fact, one of the saddest moments of the
entire movie is literally the very first scene.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:47.960
Clint Barton losing his family was originally
supposed to take place at the end of Infinity

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:48.460
War.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.620
However, the production team wisely chose
to save it for Endgame as a powerful reminder

00:07:52.639 --> 00:07:57.110
of the tragic event, and it hits audiences
right in the face and in the feels as soon

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:58.180
as the movie starts.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:07:59.680
"Boys?!

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:03.140
BOYS?!"

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:07.340
It's been established for a long time now
that the MCU version of Clint Barton is deeply

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:11.530
devoted to his family, and it's hard watching
the fear and terror mount in his face as he

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:16.140
realizes that not half but literally all of
his family has suddenly fallen to ashes.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.900
It's hands down one of the most terrible moments
of the entire film if not the MCU's whole

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:21.400
filmography.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:25.160
While the Bartons end up with a happy ending
when all is said and done, there's no doubt

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.120
that their patriarch takes the initial loss
extremely hard.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.380
Now we're in the endgame, and these last two
scenes are about as heartbreaking as any the

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:34.400
MCU has ever seen.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.840
First, we have Black Widow's death sequence.

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:41.690
Natasha Romanoff has a beautiful redemptive
arc throughout her MCU journey, and her sacrifice

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:45.920
on Vormir in the name of saving the family
she loves is one of the most perfectly tragic

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.200
ways to end her story.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:52.440
After time traveling to Vormir in 2014, Romanoff
and Barton track down the Soul Stone, only

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.020
to discover the horrible price required to
possess it.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.120
The two heroes duel each other, each striving
to be the one who makes the sacrifice.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.360
Ultimately it's Black Widow who manages to
outsmart her friend and send herself hurtling

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.120
to her death on the rocks below.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:09.710
The scene is a sad one, and it only gets deeper
knowing that her sacrifice proves instrumental

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:13.820
in saving her new family and undoing the devastation
from Thanos' snap.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:18.440
On top of that, the drama is cranked up a
notch when Barton returns home alone and delivers

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:23.200
the bad news to the despair of the team especially
Hulk, who also had a special connection with

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:23.700
the heroine.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:28.820
Of course, there's one scene in Avengers:
Endgame that stands above all of the rest.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:32.540
It's a scene larger than life, because it
involves a man who's larger than life.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:36.420
Iron Man's willingness to make the sacrifice
play and lay down on that wire to save the

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:40.760
universe is arguably the most heartbreaking
moment in the entire Infinity Saga.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.900
"I… am…

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:49.580
Iron Man."

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.720
Not only that, but the scene that follows
as Peter Parker, Pepper Potts, and others

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.860
gather round is utterly devastating.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:59.860
Pepper's final words to Tony: "We're gonna
be okay.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:00.880
You can rest now."

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.800
… is the perfect send-off to the man who
started it all.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:07.710
In addition, one of the deleted scenes shows
an extended cut of the surrounding heroes

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:12.000
taking a knee across the battlefield in honor
of the man who just gave everything to save

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:12.500
them.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:16.120
The scene is epic, beautiful, and it breaks
our hearts every time.

